Use code with caution.Task 2: Check if a String is a Palindrome(A palindrome reads the same backward as forward, like "radar" or "madam").Approach A: Two-Pointer for Loop (Traditional)This is highly optimized because it loops from both ends and stops early if a mismatch is found (\(O(1)\) extra memory).javaString input = "radar";
boolean isPalindrome = true;

int left = 0;
int right = input.length() - 1;

while (left < right) {
    if (input.charAt(left) != input.charAt(right)) {
        isPalindrome = false;
        break; // Stop immediately on mismatch
    }
    left++;
    right--;
}
System.out.println("Is Palindrome: " + isPalindrome); // Output: true
Use code with caution.Approach B: Java Streams (Functional)javaString input = "radar";
int len = input.length();

boolean isPalindrome = java.util.stream.IntStream.range(0, len / 2)
    .allMatch(i -> input.charAt(i) == input.charAt(len - 1 - i));

System.out.println("Is Palindrome: " + isPalindrome); // Output: true
Use code with caution.How the Stream works: IntStream.range(0, len / 2) generates index numbers up to the middle of the string. .allMatch() checks if the character at index i matches its mirror image character at the back end.Task 3: Reverse a StringApproach A: StringBuilder or Loop (Traditional)In interviews, if they forbid new StringBuilder(input).reverse().toString(), write this standard loop:javaString input = "stream";
String reversed = "";

for (int i = input.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    reversed += input.charAt(i); // Builds string backward
}
System.out.println("Reversed: " + reversed); // Output: maerts
Use code with caution.Approach B: Java Streams (Functional)Streams normally process from left to right, so to reverse a string, you have to stream the indices backward.javaString input = "stream";
int len = input.length();

String reversed = java.util.stream.IntStream.range(0, len)
    .mapToObj(i -> input.charAt(len - 1 - i)) // Pulls characters from back to front
    .map(String::valueOf)                     // Converts Character to String
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.joining()); // Glues them together

System.out.println("Reversed: " + reversed); // Output: maerts
Use code with caution.💡 Interview Prep TakeawaysWhen to pick Loops: Pick loops for Palindrome and Reversing algorithms. They are much easier to read, run faster, and do not create unnecessary temporary wrapper objects.When to pick Streams: Pick streams for Counting and Filtering items (like the Vowel task). The stream code is incredibly clean and declarative.Would you like to explore how to ignore spaces and case differences (like checking the phrase "Race car") in the palindrome task?yesTo check if a phrase like "Race car" or "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" is a palindrome, you must first clean the string by removing all spaces, punctuation, and uppercase differences.Here is how you handle it using both approaches.Step 1: The Core "Cleaning" StepBefore running either algorithm, you should clean the input string using Java's replaceAll() with a Regular Expression (Regex) and convert it to lowercase:javaString rawInput = "Race car";

// \\W removes all non-alphanumeric characters (spaces, commas, periods, etc.)
String cleanInput = rawInput.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]", "").toLowerCase();
// cleanInput is now completely stripped down to: "racecar"
Use code with caution.Approach A: The Traditional Two-Pointer Method (Cleaned)This is the most efficient method because it processes the string in place.javapublic class PalindromeTraditional {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String rawInput = "Race car";

        // 1. Clean the string
        String cleanInput = rawInput.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]", "").toLowerCase();

        // 2. Run the two-pointer loop
        boolean isPalindrome = true;
        int left = 0;
        int right = cleanInput.length() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            if (cleanInput.charAt(left) != cleanInput.charAt(right)) {
                isPalindrome = false;
                break; // Stop immediately if characters don't match
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        System.out.println("Is Palindrome: " + isPalindrome); // Output: true
    }
}
Use code with caution.Approach B: The Stream Method (Cleaned)With streams, you can chain the cleaning process directly into the stream logic itself.javaimport java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class PalindromeStream {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String rawInput = "Race car";

        // 1. Clean the string up front
        String cleanInput = rawInput.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]", "").toLowerCase();
        int len = cleanInput.length();

        // 2. Stream the indices to check mirror matches
        boolean isPalindrome = IntStream.range(0, len / 2)
            .allMatch(i -> cleanInput.charAt(i) == cleanInput.charAt(len - 1 - i));

        System.out.println("Is Palindrome: " + isPalindrome); // Output: true
    }
}
Use code with caution.💡 Interview Pro-Tip: Advanced Stream AlternativeIf an interviewer asks you to clean the string inside the stream without using replaceAll(), you can filter out characters using Character.isLetterOrDigit() like this:javaString rawInput = "Race car";

// Extract, filter, and normalize entirely inside a stream pipeline
String cleanInput = rawInput.chars()
    .filter(Character::isLetterOrDigit)
    .map(Character::toLowerCase)
    .collect(StringBuilder::new, StringBuilder::appendCodePoint, StringBuilder::append)
    .toString();
